        "prompt": "Animate this SAME rear-view Ash Warden performing ONE powerful TWO-HANDED BASEBALL-STYLE GREATSWORD SWING, then recovery. Camera stays low top-down behind him: he faces NORTH AWAY FROM THE VIEWER, back of helmet and broad back armor remain visible, NO front face or visor facing the camera. Start from this supplied rear combat stance with the sword low to viewer right. Coil hips, chest, BOTH shoulders and elbows into a clear side backswing, shift weight over planted wide boots. Uncoil with the WHOLE BODY and both hands driving ONE huge broad sword across the front of the character (away from camera) in a LARGE committed lateral arc, not a tiny wrist motion. Continue through to a HIGH FOLLOW-THROUGH beside or above the OPPOSITE VIEWER-LEFT SHOULDER, torso visibly twisted after the strike, one supporting boot pivots. Then smoothly return the sword and both hands to the original low-right rear idle stance. Preserve the force, broad amplitude, inertia and full windup-strike-follow-through-recovery of a heavy baseball swing. Keep both gauntlets on ONE handle throughout; only one thick heavy greatsword. Same charcoal armor, massive shoulder plates, moderate dry branches, dead roots and torn ash tabard seen from BEHIND. No full spin toward camera, no overhead DOWNWARD ground slam, no jump or running, no fire projection, ground fissure, particles or scenery. Entire figure and blade inside the canvas.",
